This is a collection of YANIs by users of the #nethack IRC channel on
freenode.net. Please excuse any old ideas that may be present.

[1]: Rock traps aren't strong enough in later levels. Implement falling
boulder traps. Due to the large size of boulders, they can only hold
one. --JoshJ

[2]: If you try to move onto a square occupied by a tame monster that
can engulf, it should engulf you, and moving again expels you in the
direction you moved. --glassbeat

Uh... I would hope that my pet purple worm would know better than to
eat me when I try to get by it.

[3]: Items should flash (BUC status) when items fall on an altar from
monster death, monster drops, teleportation, etc. --assended

This seems to me to be mostly a benefit to farmers.  Is that what we
want?

[4]: A cursed magic whistle should teleport random monsters on the
level to you. Or your pet away from you. --assended

[5]: The other three corners of the castle should have mimics
pretending to be chests. --aioli

Heh heh!  That's evil.  How about a mix of giant mimics and trapped
chests?

[6]: The castle should have some kind of powerful unique, akin of
Croesus. He should not be dumb enough to step onto the portcullis.
--aioli

[7]: Leprochauns should be able to turn straw golems into gold golems.
--assended

Uhm.... I thought that was Rumplestitskin.  There are no
Rumplestiltskins in the game that I'm aware of.  If there were any,
then they definitely should have this ability.  Was Rumplestiltskin a
gnome?  I guess there could be a random unique gnome named
Rumplestiltskin that has this ability, but how often would this come up
if so?

[8]: greased scrolls and spellbooks should not go blank from dipping
but should become non-writable once blank. --assended

That would be _too_ realistic.  And who greases their scrolls?

[9]: Items that are used in actions requiring confirmation (i.e. corpse
sacrifice) should avoid being assigned to item letter 'y' and 'Y' to
avoid accidental #pray + y instead of #offer + y. (idea by assended)
--Eidolos

Sounds like a non-trivial coding task to me, but what do I know.

[10]: New creature inhabiting some gehennom levels, afraid of light
sources but can cause instadeath. Upon arriving on a level with such a
creature, one might get the message "It is dark in here. You might be
eaten by a grue." Makes Sunsword more valuable, and saving a magic lamp
or saving potions of oil and an oil lamp. --Yui

More instadeath!?  Why not just YAFM when you enter a dark level
without a light source?

[11]: If you're a canine, you should not see in color. --BenUrban

THAT is funny!  Sounds like a PITA for the coder and the player,
though.  If anyone wants to write it, I'd consider installing it
locally, myself... but I'm weird.  Actually, don't they think that most
animals have little color vision?  I think bees see in
black/white+ultraviolet, for instance.

[12]: Projectile weapons that succumb to 'disappearing' should remain
in the game but as 'broken foo'. Wielding them and reading enchant
armour will repair them (but without prior enchantment if it's too
powerful) --Patashu

[13]: Hamlet had poisoned daggers, swords, etc. Nethack could too.
--azgor

Well, I was told this was YAOI actually.  And when I thought about it,
I had the following observations:

Permanent poisoned projectiles?  Preposterous proposition, probably!
Previous prevarications purportedly prove prospect presents problems.

Still, this is an RPG!  There should be an element of consistency to
make the game more immersive and realistic.  Poisoned arrows? Poisoned
darts? Poisoned swords!  Maybe the trick is to have the poison wear off
eventually so you can't have a blessed greased fixed poisoned +7 silver
saber called Greyswandir for half the game.  You wouldn't notice the
poison wearing off of arrows/darts/etc. because they are used up before
the poison has a chance to wear off.  If it's YAOI and has already been
discussed to death, then nevermind.  I guess my contribution is only to
draw attention to prior art.

[14]: A shield skill which, when at skilled/expert, will have a chance
of blocking an attack completely, or maybe halving it. --Chilliwack

[15]: Werecockatrice (need I say more?) --assended

Uh...  I should start editing these lists before I post them.  I don't
currently, because when I contemplated doing so early on, I found
